    gmeow:useWhen "Use for rotation about the vertical axis in the Euler-angle orientation facet."@x-gmeow-english .

<https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/places>
    a owl:Ontology ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow> ;
    rdfs:comment """Locations — geographic places, online (virtual) locations, digital storage locations, celestial / astronomical locations, and biological-sequence loci — with coordinates, geometry, containment, and external-gazetteer coreference (the schema.org / GeoSPARQL / GeoNames / Getty TGN / Wikidata / WGS84 / BOT / ifcOWL / IVOA / UAT / SWEET / FALDO / Sequence Ontology superset layer). Place kinds, celestial object kinds, and sequence feature kinds are open value vocabularies (gmeow:placeType, gmeow:celestialObjectType, gmeow:sequenceFeatureType), so any granularity from country to room, from star to galaxy cluster, or from chromosome to SNP, can be a first-class, QID-bearing entity chained by containedInPlace or locatedAt.

STANDPOINT DOCTRINE. Contested facts in the locations slice — sovereignty/jurisdiction disputes, competing place names (endonym vs exonym per community), boundary changes, regulatory overlays — are standpoint-indexed claims that COEXIST, none privileged (Principle 9). A contested sovereignty claim (e.g. Crimea-class) is two gmeow:accordingTo-annotated gmeow:containedInPlace base triples. A contested toponym is two gmeow:accordingTo-annotated gmeow:hasPlaceName statements, or two reified gmeow:PlaceNaming instances each carrying gmeow:accordingTo. gmeow:usageAuthority on a gmeow:NameUsage names the toponymic authority; gmeow:accordingTo on the same usage names the standpoint frame asserting it — the two axes are orthogonal and must not be conflated. There is NO location-specific dispute mechanism, no preferredJurisdiction, no primaryName — only the cross-cutting standpoint facility. Superseded or withdrawn claims set gmeow:displayable false, never deletion (Principle 10).

REGULATORY OVERLAYS (regulatory-overlay design + vertical/maritime/aviation-zone design). Legal and regulatory spatial overlays — zoning, protected areas, restricted airspace, sanctions, tax/electoral districts, postal zones, civil-time zones, fishing zones, customs zones, maritime zones (territorial sea, EEZ, continental shelf, high seas, marine protected area), aviation zones (airway, CTR, TMA, FIR, ATZ, MOA, warning, alert), and temporary NOTAM notices — are modelled as reified gmeow:RegulatoryOverlay instances (⊑ gmeow:TimeScopedRelation), each binding a place, an authority, an overlay type (open value vocabulary), and an optional RightsStatement (rights facility) for the deontic rules. 3D bounds (altitude/depth) are frame-relative math:Quantity values in explicit vertical reference frames (Principle 11). Contested overlays (disputed EEZ, overlapping airspace claims) coexist as multiple standpoint-indexed instances, never a single winner (Principle 9).

VIRTUAL LOCATIONS + NETWORK ADDRESS SPACE (virtual-location design). Virtual locations (video conferences, chat spaces, websites, social media pages) are typed by an open value vocabulary (gmeow:VirtualLocationType). Their network addresses — IP, IPv6, MAC, DNS, URL, port, BGP-AS — are modelled as frame-relative coordinates (gmeow:NetworkAddress) in topology-only reference frames (gmeow:frameKindTopological) nested under the Internet root frame via gmeow:parentFrame. CIDR containment, DNS resolution, and path-length are solver-layer computations (Principle 12), never asserted as triples in the OWL core.

MATHEMATICAL / n-D REFERENCE FRAMES (mathematical-frame design). Hilbert spaces, smooth manifolds, phase spaces, and latent vector spaces are first-class gmeow:ReferenceFrame instances under gmeow:frameRealmMathematical. A finite-dimensional vector space (ℝⁿ, ℂⁿ, latent embedding) uses one gmeow:Axis with gmeow:hasCoordinateMatrix encoding the full column/row matrix via gmeow:CoordinateMatrix.matrixShape, not n separate axis individuals (Principle 12 — the ontology carries structure, solver carries cardinality). A configuration space (C-space, e.g. 6-DOF robot arm joint angles) uses one axis per joint. A phase space uses paired generalized-coordinate and generalized-momentum axes (qᵢ, pᵢ) and carries gmeow:hasMetricKind gmeow:metricSymplectic. A Hilbert space (∞-D) and latent vector spaces are modelled with gmeow:dimensionCount 1 and a single axis, leaving actual dimensionality to the matrix shape attribute, since the ontology does not assert infinite-dimensional structures as OWL individuals (Principle 12). OpenMath / Content-MathML encoding of coordinates is handled by the value-space layer (gmeow:coordinateValue typed by gmeow:CoordinateEncoding), not by per-expression ontology axioms.

ROBOTIC REALM — C-SPACE / TF / SLAM (robotic-frame design). Robot configuration spaces (C-space) are modelled as gmeow:ReferenceFrame instances in the gmeow:frameRealmRobotic realm, with joint-angle axes and geodesic metrics on a configuration-space manifold. Task-space and velocity frames are Cartesian seeds under the same realm. The TF transform tree is a hierarchy of nested frames linked via gmeow:parentFrame; static vs dynamic transforms are distinguished by statement-level temporal metadata (statement DSL), not by new ontology properties — the actual homogeneous-transform matrix math lives in the solver layer (Principle 12). SLAM occupancy maps are probabilistic occupancy-grid reference frames hosted by the robot, with gmeow:determinacyProbabilistic and gmeow:confidence carried on the observation results (CoordinateMatrix or SpatialCoordinates), not on the frame itself.

BIOLOGICAL-SEQUENCE REALM — FALDO / SEQUENCE ONTOLOGY / GFF3 (biological-sequence design). Biological sequences (DNA, RNA, protein) are modelled as gmeow:ReferenceFrame instances in the gmeow:frameRealmBiological realm. A reference assembly (e.g. GRCh38) is a versioned, linear 1-D reference frame with a single position axis; a sequence feature (gene, exon, SNP, CDS) is a gmeow:BiologicalSequenceLocation bearing gmeow:SequenceCoordinates with explicit start, end, and strand in that frame. Feature types are an open value vocabulary (gmeow:sequenceFeatureType) aligned by reference to Sequence Ontology (SO) terms. FALDO positions map to gmeow:SequenceCoordinates; liftover (coordinate conversion between assemblies) is declared via gmeow:transformsTo on the ReferenceFrame but executed by an external solver (Principle 12).

CADASTRAL / LAND ADMINISTRATION REALM — LADM / INSPIRE (cadastral design). Parcels, lots, and spatial units are Places typed by an open value vocabulary (gmeow:placeType). Their boundaries are Geometry / SpatialCoordinates in a terrestrial reference frame. Ownership and land rights (ownership, lease, easement, mortgage, usufruct) are modelled as reified gmeow:LandTenure instances (⊑ gmeow:TimeScopedRelation), each binding a place, a party (Agent), a rights statement (rights facility), and a tenure type (open value vocabulary). A structured cadastral identifier (folio, title, lot, parcel ID) is modelled as a reified gmeow:CadastralReference (⊑ gmeow:InformationObject) linked to the place. Boundary surveys carry determinacy (typically crisp for surveyed boundaries, disputed for contested claims). Contested titles coexist as multiple standpoint-indexed LandTenures, none privileged (Principle 9). Expired or lapsed tenures keep their record with gmeow:displayable false, never deletion (Principle 10). Area and intersection computations stay in the solver layer (Principle 12). Aligns ISO 19152 LADM and INSPIRE Cadastral Parcels by reference (SSSOM)."""@x-gmeow-english ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/places> ;
    rdfs:label "GMEOW Locations Module"@x-gmeow-english ;
    skos:definition "The GMEOW Locations Module — classes, properties, and individuals of the GMEOW super-vocabulary."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxConfigBox ,
        gmeow:boxTBox .

_:b0
    a owl:AllDisjointProperties ;
    owl:members ( gmeow:rcc8dc gmeow:rcc8ec gmeow:rcc8po gmeow:rcc8tpp gmeow:rcc8ntpp gmeow:rcc8tppi gmeow:rcc8ntppi gmeow:rcc8eq ) .

# Closed-world cardinality/value axioms backing the hand-authored SHACL
# well-formedness shapes. Observation results remain multi-valued so measurements
# in distinct frames or from coexisting standpoints do not collapse.
gmeow:ProximityMeasurement rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty gmeow:observedFeature ; owl:maxQualifiedCardinality 1 ; owl:onClass owl:Thing ] ,
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty gmeow:proximityTo ; owl:maxQualifiedCardinality 1 ; owl:onClass owl:Thing ] .

# exact-one minCount restoration (issue #1194): min-qualified sibling restrictions
# restoring sh:minCount for the reasoner-safe max-qualified exact-one antecedents.
<https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/ProximityMeasurement> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> [ <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> ; <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/observedFeature> ; <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#minQualifiedCardinality> "1"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> ; <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onClass> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing> ] .
<https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/ProximityMeasurement> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> [ <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> ; <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/proximityTo> ; <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#minQualifiedCardinality> "1"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> ; <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onClass> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing> ] .
<https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/ProximityMeasurement> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> [ <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Restriction> ; <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onProperty> <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/observationResult> ; <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#minQualifiedCardinality> "1"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> ; <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#onClass> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing> ] .

# =========================================================================== #
# Reference-frame profile constraints (#1194): the procedural fragment of the   #
# hand-authored gmeow:FrameProfileShape, migrated to compact logic: records     #
# projected to sh:SPARQLConstraint keyed (via logic:formalizes) onto            #
# gmeow:ReferenceFrame. The declarative cardinality/presence fragment is the     #
# frame realm/kind/axis stack; these two carry the value-typing of hasAxis and   #
# the dimension-count agreement the shape enforced procedurally.                 #
# =========================================================================== #

# Every gmeow:hasAxis value of a gmeow:ReferenceFrame is a gmeow:Axis — a
# coordinate axis reference that is not a typed axis is ill-formed.
gmeow:FrameAxisTypeConstraint
    a logic:PathValueTypeConstraint ;
    rdfs:label "Reference-frame axis type constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/places> ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    skos:definition "A closed-world integrity constraint: every gmeow:hasAxis value of a gmeow:ReferenceFrame is a gmeow:Axis (a coordinate axis)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:onClass gmeow:ReferenceFrame ;
    logic:valuePath gmeow:hasAxis ;
    logic:valueClass gmeow:Axis ;
    logic:formalizes gmeow:ReferenceFrame , gmeow:FrameProfileShape ;
    logic:message "A reference frame's coordinate axis (gmeow:hasAxis) must be a gmeow:Axis." .

# A reference frame's declared gmeow:dimensionCount must equal the number of
# distinct coordinate axes it carries through gmeow:hasAxis (an aggregate
# COUNT(DISTINCT) agreement).
gmeow:FrameDimensionCountConstraint
    a logic:AggregateConstraint ;
    rdfs:label "Reference-frame dimension-count constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/places> ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    skos:definition "A closed-world integrity constraint: a gmeow:ReferenceFrame's gmeow:dimensionCount equals the number of distinct gmeow:hasAxis coordinate axes it carries."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:onClass gmeow:ReferenceFrame ;
    logic:aggFunction "COUNT" ;
    logic:aggDistinct true ;
    logic:aggPath gmeow:hasAxis ;
    logic:aggComparator "=" ;
    logic:aggCompareTo gmeow:dimensionCount ;
    logic:formalizes gmeow:ReferenceFrame , gmeow:FrameProfileShape ;
    logic:message "A reference frame's dimension count must equal its number of coordinate axes." .

# =========================================================================== #
# Suppression constraint (#1194) — projection of SupersededPlaceSuppressionShape.
# A superseded place (one carrying gmeow:placeSupersededBy) that is not          #
# suppressed (gmeow:displayable false) is a Warning — a source may lag the flag. #
# =========================================================================== #

gmeow:SupersededPlaceSuppressionConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:label "Superseded place suppression constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/places> ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    skos:definition "A closed-world integrity constraint: a superseded gmeow:Place (one carrying gmeow:placeSupersededBy) should set gmeow:displayable false — suppressed from display, never deleted (Principle 10)."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:formalizes gmeow:Place , gmeow:SupersededPlaceSuppressionShape ;
    logic:severity "Warning" ;
    logic:message "A superseded place (one with gmeow:placeSupersededBy) should set gmeow:displayable false — suppressed from display, never deleted." ;
    logic:integrity logic:supPlForall .
logic:supPlForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:supPlImpl .
logic:supPlImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:supPlGuard ; logic:consequent logic:supPlPhi .
logic:supPlGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:relation <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri gmeow:Place ] .
logic:supPlPhi a logic:Formula ; logic:not logic:supPlEx .
logic:supPlEx a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "s" ] ;
    logic:exists logic:supPlBody .
logic:supPlBody a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:supPlSuc , logic:supPlNotSuppressed .
logic:supPlSuc a logic:Formula ; logic:relation gmeow:placeSupersededBy ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "s" ] .
logic:supPlNotSuppressed a logic:Formula ; logic:not logic:supPlDisp .
logic:supPlDisp a logic:Formula ; logic:relation gmeow:displayable ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termLiteral "false" ; logic:termLiteralDatatype xsd:boolean ] .

# =========================================================================== #
# Migrated procedural constraint (#1194): coarsenTo target-level reachability   #
# (Principle 10 disclosure control), from gmeow:CoarsenToShape (Warning).        #
# =========================================================================== #
gmeow:CoarsenToReachabilityConstraint
    a logic:Constraint ;
    rdfs:label "coarsenTo target-level reachability constraint"@x-gmeow-english ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/places> ;
    gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox ;
    skos:definition "A closed-world reachability constraint: for every gmeow:coarsenTo target level, some gmeow:containedInPlace+ ancestor carries a matching gmeow:hasGranularity, so the coarsened value has a replacement."@x-gmeow-english ;
    logic:formalizes gmeow:CoarsenToShape ;
    logic:severity "Warning" ;
    logic:message "A gmeow:coarsenTo target level is not reachable: no gmeow:containedInPlace+ ancestor carries a matching gmeow:hasGranularity, so the value would be suppressed with no coarser replacement." ;
    logic:integrity logic:ctrForall .
logic:ctrForall a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "lvl" ] ;
    logic:forall logic:ctrImpl .
logic:ctrImpl a logic:Formula ; logic:antecedent logic:ctrGuard ; logic:consequent logic:ctrPhi .
logic:ctrGuard a logic:Formula ; logic:relation gmeow:coarsenTo ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "lvl" ] .
logic:ctrPhi a logic:Formula ;
    logic:quantifiedVariable [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "ancestor" ] ;
    logic:exists logic:ctrBody .
logic:ctrBody a logic:Formula ; logic:and logic:ctrReach , logic:ctrGran .
logic:ctrReach a logic:Formula ; logic:relation logic:transitiveReach ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "this" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termIri gmeow:containedInPlace ] , [ logic:termIndex 2 ; logic:termVariable "ancestor" ] .
logic:ctrGran a logic:Formula ; logic:relation gmeow:hasGranularity ;
    logic:argument [ logic:termIndex 0 ; logic:termVariable "ancestor" ] , [ logic:termIndex 1 ; logic:termVariable "lvl" ] .

